---
/**
 * Homepage. Copy is the owner's v3 draft, supplied 14 Aug 2026: first person
 * throughout, no em dashes in anything a visitor reads, no case studies and no
 * invented proof. The hero and the plates were explicitly out of scope for
 * that rewrite and are unchanged.
 *
 * Two sections from the previous structure are gone. §04 sold the free systems
 * map, which the owner killed as an offer on the same date. §05 was Work, and
 * it is not coming back until there is a real project to put in it — the
 * owner's own note: one real client with one real number will outperform every
 * sentence on this page.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ConnectsStrip from '../components/ConnectsStrip.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Fig2CurrentState from '../components/Fig2CurrentState.astro';
import Fig3ThreadDiagram from '../components/Fig3ThreadDiagram.astro';
import Fig4CarePlate from '../components/Fig4CarePlate.astro';
import StepRow from '../components/StepRow.astro';
import ServiceList from '../components/ServiceList.astro';
import QuestionList from '../components/QuestionList.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Base businessSchema>
  <Header />
  <Hero />
  <ConnectsStrip />

  <Section
    eyebrow="§ 01 · The problem"
    heading="Your software works. It just doesn’t work together."
    headingWidth={20}
    headingGap={24}
    noteLabel="Worth asking"
  >
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      QuickBooks does the accounting. Square takes the payments. The scheduling app
      schedules. Every tool you bought does its job, and not one of them knows the others
      exist.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      So a person becomes the connection. Someone reads a number off one screen and types
      it into another. Someone opens four tabs to answer one question. Someone remembers to
      send the invoice, until the week they don’t.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      That work never shows up on a report, which is why it never gets fixed. It’s also the
      first thing I remove.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      What does a customer touch, from first contact to final payment? Which of those steps
      happens because a person <strong>remembers</strong> to do it? Most businesses can’t
      answer the first question from a single screen. That gap is the work.
    </Fragment>

    <Figure slot="after" caption="Current state, typical intake">
      <Fig2CurrentState />
    </Figure>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="services"
    eyebrow="§ 02 · What I do"
    heading="Connect what you have. Build what you don’t."
    headingWidth={18}
    noteLabel="Which one you need"
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:52ch;margin-bottom:22px">
      Most businesses need some of both.
    </p>

    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>Your tools are fine. The gaps between them aren’t.</strong> You chose good
      software for good reasons and you don’t want to replace any of it. So I don’t. I build
      what runs between them: the dashboard that pulls from all of it, the automation that
      moves the data, the handoffs that stop depending on someone remembering. Nothing gets
      ripped out, and your team keeps working the way they already know how.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      <strong>The tool you need isn’t for sale.</strong> Every business has a process no
      product fits, so it lives in a spreadsheet, a group chat, and one person’s head. I
      build that piece properly: shaped like your actual process, connected to everything
      else you run, and built to outlast the person currently holding it together.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      If it already connects to anything, I can connect to it. Accounting, payments, point
      of sale, scheduling, inventory, email, whatever you’re running. The first call is
      where we find out what’s possible with your particular stack.
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="after">
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:52ch;margin-top:8px">
        Either way you end up with one system instead of seven, and you own it.
      </p>

      <Figure caption="The thread, drawn">
        <Fig3ThreadDiagram />
      </Figure>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="how"
    eyebrow="§ 03 · How it works"
    heading="Three steps. You see it working before you’ve spent most of the money."
    headingWidth={24}
  >
    <StepRow />
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="own"
    eyebrow="§ 04 · What you own"
    heading="Custom means yours. I mean that literally."
    headingWidth={17}
    noteLabel="Worth asking"
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:54ch;margin-bottom:22px">
      Most software you buy, you rent. The price goes up at renewal, your data lives on
      someone else’s server, and the day you stop paying, you stop operating. This is the
      opposite of that.
    </p>

    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>It runs in your accounts.</strong> Your cloud, your database, your domain,
      your billing. I build it there from day one.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>You keep the source code.</strong> Handed over with documentation at the end
      of every project, with full commercial rights. Not escrowed, not licensed back to
      you.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>Your data stays yours.</strong> I don’t sell a product, so there’s no product
      for your data to sit inside. Nothing pooled, nothing resold, nothing used to train
      anything.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>No per-seat pricing.</strong> Hire your tenth employee, or your fiftieth. The
      software costs what it cost.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      <strong>You can replace me.</strong> Any competent developer can pick up what I built,
      because it’s documented and it isn’t exotic. If I stop being useful you lose a vendor,
      not your operations.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      Worth asking any software company you currently pay: what happens to my business the
      day I cancel?
    </Fragment>

    <Figure slot="after" caption="What ships with every build" narrow>
      <Fig4CarePlate />
    </Figure>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="pricing"
    eyebrow="§ 05 · Pricing"
    heading="Fixed scope. Fixed price. Agreed before I start."
    headingWidth={19}
    noteLabel="On AI"
    docGap={44}
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:52ch">
      No hourly billing and no change orders. If I get the estimate wrong, that’s mine to
      absorb.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      I use it where it holds up. Sorting and routing incoming work, drafting the messages
      you send fifty times a month, pulling data out of documents, flagging what needs a
      person to look at it. It shows up inside the work rather than as something you buy
      separately, because on its own it isn’t the answer to anything.
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="after">
      <ServiceList />

      {/* PRICES DELIBERATELY ABSENT. The v3 draft carried $14,000, $28,000 and
          $2,400/month and its own call-out marked all three as placeholders.
          Owner confirmed 14 Aug 2026: durations and terms only until the real
          figures exist. Do not fill these in. See CLAUDE.md. */}
      <p class="txt" style="margin-top:34px;max-width:52ch">
        <strong>My guarantee.</strong> Two weeks in, you’ll have working software in your
        hands. If it’s clear by then that this isn’t going to work, either of us can stop
        and you owe nothing beyond that point. I’d rather lose two weeks than build
        something you don’t use.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="questions"
    eyebrow="§ 06 · Questions"
    heading="The fair ones, answered plainly."
    headingWidth={14}
  >
    <Fragment slot="after">
      <QuestionList />
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="about"
    eyebrow="§ 07 · About"
    heading="Built in Hawaiʻi. Useful anywhere."
    headingWidth={17}
    noteLabel="Working with a small practice"
  >
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      Thread is me. I build custom software for businesses across the islands, and for
      clients on the mainland who’d rather talk to the person writing the code than to an
      account manager.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      Being here shapes the work. Island businesses run lean, on tools chosen years ago for
      good reasons, with no appetite for tearing it all out and starting over. That
      constraint turned out to be the right way to build software for anyone.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      The name is the whole idea. A thread runs through everything and holds the separate
      pieces together as one thing.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      I take a few projects at a time, which is the point. You get the person who
      understands your business, not whoever was available that week. It also means the
      calendar fills, so if the timing matters to you, say so early.
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <ContactSection />
  <Footer />
</Base>
